/* A field guide to decision-making structures — content data.
   Scenarios are illustrative unless a case is listed on the Evidence page.
   Loop diagrams are this guide's own models of mechanisms described in the cited research. */
window.FG = {};

FG.families = [
  {id:"1", name:"Deciding by authority", sub:"One person or a few decide for everyone",
   text:"The simplest structure is one person making the call, like a principal for a whole-school decision. Variations add a step first, such as asking for advice, or pass orders down through layers of managers. The decision can also sit with a small group, chosen for expertise, for seniority or by lot. These structures are fast, and they work well when the deciders already know what they need to know.",
   school:"A school owner setting fees, a principal setting the timetable, a trust board approving the budget, or a head teacher hiring after a panel interview.",
   agree:"Here, agreement is mostly about whether people go along with the decision. People below the decider often stay quiet even when they disagree, and advisers tend to repeat what the most senior person has said. So silence can look like agreement when it isn't.",
   forces:["spiral_silence","conformity","pluralistic_ignorance"]},
  {id:"3", name:"Deciding by consensus", sub:"The group works toward a decision everyone can support, or at least live with",
   text:"Consent asks “can you live with it?” where a vote asks “which do you prefer?”. A proposal goes ahead unless someone raises a reasoned objection, and the group uses the objection to improve the proposal. Consensus goes further and keeps talking until everyone can support the decision. Both take more time than a vote, and both usually come with a date to review the decision. At the informal end, a small group of friends may simply agree with no rules at all, which works until the group grows. Many set a fallback vote in case agreement never comes. A whole community can also reach agreement in an open meeting, where raised hands show support without anyone counting them.",
   school:"A Grade 7 team trying a homework policy for six weeks, or a curriculum committee that approves changes unless someone objects by Friday.",
   agree:"Agreement here means no one sees a serious problem, which is easier to reach than everyone liking the same option. The risk is that people with less power, like students or new staff, don't feel safe objecting, and their silence gets counted as consent.",
   forces:["spiral_silence","influence_few","groupthink"]},
  {id:"2", name:"Deciding by voting", sub:"Each person votes, and a rule turns the votes into a decision",
   text:"In a vote, each person picks an option and a rule turns the count into a decision. Small choices in the rule matter a lot: whether you need more than half or two-thirds, how you count when there are more than two options, and whether people vote in the open or in private. Some rules also change what a vote is worth, by weighting it by stake, letting people spend more votes on what they care about most, or letting support build up over time.",
   school:"A student council election, a staff vote at 4 pm, a parent association voting on the school start time, or a school network where bigger schools get more votes.",
   agree:"Votes look independent, but people influence each other before they vote. In a show of hands, early hands sway the people who vote later. When votes are weighted, a few large voters can end up deciding for everyone.",
   forces:["cascade","conformity","influence_few"]},
  {id:"7", name:"No one in charge", sub:"Groups with no leader and no vote",
   text:"Some groups have no one in charge. Without agreed rules, the best-connected people end up deciding, often unnoticed. Nature shows another way: bee swarms, herds, ant trails and slime moulds decide well with no leader, because each individual follows a few simple shared rules. These are not structures a school would adopt whole, but each suggests a practical design for how people can search, copy and settle on a choice.",
   school:"Five teachers planning a picnic in a chat group, a parent group with no committee, staff each visiting possible sites before backing one, or students wearing paths across a new campus.",
   agree:"Groups without a leader choose well when individuals search on their own before they copy each other. When copying starts too early, the group can lock onto a poor choice.",
   forces:["network_shape","influence_few","cascade","cumulative"]},
  {id:"8", name:"Shaping proposals", sub:"Methods that improve what gets decided, before anyone decides",
   text:"These methods don't make the decision. They shape the proposal it is made from: who gets heard, in what order, and whether people think on their own before they hear the most senior voice. Any of them can come before any structure on this page. A committee might gather ideas on sticky notes, test the plan with a pre-mortem, and then decide it by vote.",
   school:"Teachers writing ideas alone before a staff meeting, students sorting sticky notes into themes for a school fair, or a head talking to each department before proposing a new timetable.",
   agree:"Many poor group decisions go wrong before the decision, when people copy whoever speaks first. These methods protect independent judgement, so different people's mistakes cancel out instead of piling up.",
   forces:["independent","conformity","cascade","shared_info"]}
];

